<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kefalonia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cephalonia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kefalonia (Cephalonia) is the largest of the Ionian Islands, off the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Byzantine until 1185, it was then ruled by Norman and Italian counts palatine, briefly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> from 1500 to 1797. After the Septinsular Republic and British protection it joined Greece in 1864, and it was devastated by an earthquake in 1953.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kefalonia.jpg" alt="Kefalonia" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matt Sims · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asos,_Kefalonia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kefalonia, also written Cephalonia, is the largest of the Ionian Islands and the sixth-largest island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and Chios. The main island covers 773 square kilometres. Its highest mountain is Mount Ainos, at 1,628 metres, whose summit is covered with Greek fir and black pine and is a national park. Argostoli and Lixouri are its largest towns. The island lies just south-east of a major active fault zone where the Eurasian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> plates meet, and a series of thrust faults is responsible for its continuing uplift.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological finds on the island go back to about 40,000 years before the present, and a survey in the Leivatho area has found dozens of sites from the Palaeolithic to the Venetian period. The Mycenaean era is represented in the small archaeological museum of Argostoli. In antiquity the people lived in four cities, Cranii, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sami-people">Sami</a>, Pale and Pronnoi, which formed a federation called the tetrapolis. Legend explained the island's name through Cephalus, who helped Amphitryon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> in a war and was rewarded with the island of Same.</p><p>In the late Roman Empire Cephalonia belonged to the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, and the four ancient cities survived into late antiquity, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sami-people">Sami</a> probably the capital. In 1222 the Frankish Crusaders established the Diocese of Kefalonia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a>. Turkish rule lasted until 1500, when a Spanish-Venetian army captured the island, a rare Venetian success in the Second Ottoman-Venetian War. Refugees from the lost Venetian fortresses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koroni">Coron</a> and colonists from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> arrived, and Venetian pressure for olive plantations led to the first exports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1797, Cephalonia became part of the French département of Ithaque. In 1798 a combined Russian and Turkish fleet forced the French out, and from 1799 to 1807 the island belonged to the Septinsular Republic, nominally under Ottoman sovereignty but protected by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In 1864 it joined the Greek state with the other Ionian Islands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In the Second World War Axis forces occupied the island, mostly Italian troops until late 1943. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s armistice in September 1943 the Italian division Acqui, having voted in a referendum among its soldiers, fought the German forces that came to take control, which prevailed after the siege of Argostoli. The earthquake of 1953 was so destructive that of 200 olive presses working before it, thirteen remain. Argostoli is the seat of administration, and Cephalonia is a regional unit of the Ionian Islands region. Tourism, begun in the early 19th century, grew widely from the 1980s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cephalonia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1185 the Norman admiral Margaritus of Brindisi took <b>Cephalonia</b> and Zakynthos from Byzantium for William II of Sicily.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-cephalonia-and-zakynthos">County Palatine of Cephalonia and Zakynthos</a></li><li><blockquote>The Orsini of <b>Cephalonia</b> took over in 1318.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-epirus">Despotate of Epirus</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Constantinople in 1800 created the Septinsular Republic of Corfu, Paxos, Lefkada, Ithaca, <b>Cephalonia</b>, Zakynthos and Kythira, under Ottoman suzerainty and Russian protection.</blockquote><a href="/state/septinsular-republic">Septinsular Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>It later added Zakynthos (1485), <b>Cephalonia</b> and Ithaca (1500) and finally Lefkada (1684), while Kythira was already Venetian, gaining control over the sea route between the Adriatic and the eastern Mediterranean.</blockquote><a href="/state/venetian-ionian-islands">Venetian Ionian Islands</a></li></ul></section>
